// Cut from the dumping ground I built.
//
// ITER.260822.04 anchored three personas to FRAME.260819.01 and recorded a decision saying
// the falsifier was "a fourth persona". The falsifier fired in the same iteration and
// differently: the frame went from one surface to four, and the reason is structural rather
// than numeric. The frame is the ROOT. Every surface descends from it by construction, so
// `serves "FRAME.260819.01"` is true of everything and discriminates nothing.
//
// The fix is not a rule that detects the pile. no-dumping-grounds — this plugin's oldest
// invariant — forbids the NAMES utils, helpers, common, shared. It never measures whether a
// folder became a junk drawer. Anti-dumping works by making the dumping ground unnameable,
// and the same move applies to an edge: remove `frame` from the vocabulary.
//
// Which leaves the personas needing somewhere real to point, and that turns out to be worth
// having for its own sake.

thin-slice "TS.260821.08" {
    slug "hold-who-may-attest-what"
    title "Refuse a close whose reviewer is the agent that did the work"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.claim-settlement"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "close"
    actor "the agent closing an iteration"
    produces-event "SelfApprovalRefused" "AttestationAccepted"
    needs-read-model "what-this-iteration-claims-and-has-shown"

    trigger "an iteration closes, and the only thing standing between it and self-attestation is a sentence in a Markdown file"
    outcome "who may attest what is a record the engine reads, and an attestation by the identity that produced the work is refused"

    scenario #"""
        Given an iteration whose phases record who worked them, and a role vocabulary saying
             which phases each role owns and what it may sign
        When the iteration is closed
        Then a close attested by the identity that worked the `implement` phase is REFUSED —
             because an artifact looks identical whether the reviewer was a different mind or the
             same one wearing a second hat, and that is S2 stated in prose in two files and
             computed nowhere.
        """#

    step "1" does="declare `role` — who, which phases it owns, what it may sign, and what it may never sign for itself"
    step "2" does="a phase records who worked it. The record already holds `by` on trail entries and nothing on phases"
    step "3" does="close refuses when the attesting identity worked a phase the attestation covers"
    step "4" does="remove `frame` from the `serves` vocabulary — with roles declared, nothing needs it"
    step "5" does="the three persona files serve the roles they describe, and using-praxis serves multi-harness-distribution, which owns RouterInjected"

    excludes "deciding WHO a human is. The record holds `human:<name>` from git identity and that \
        is as far as identity goes here — an engine that adjudicates whether two names are one \
        person is an engine with an opinion about employment"
    excludes "refusing an agent from working two phases. Doing the design and the implementation is \
        normal and often better; what is refused is ATTESTING your own work, which is a different act"
    excludes "a `phase-kind` vocabulary. The five stages are prose on the phase entity today and \
        that is a separate weakness — fixing it here would make this slice about the schema \
        rather than about attestation"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="a persona file says which role it is, and the role says what that means to the engine"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="close refuses a self-attested iteration, and `serves` can no longer name the frame"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`who checked this` has an answer that is not `the person who wrote it`"

    claim "C1" text="a close attested by the identity that worked a covered phase is refused" \
        settled-by="a test closing an iteration whose implement phase and whose attestation carry one identity, asserting a refusal"
    claim "C2" text="the same identity working two phases is NOT refused — only attesting its own work is" \
        settled-by="a test with one identity across design-system and implement and a different attester, asserting the close succeeds"
    claim "C3" text="`frame` is not a `serves` target, so the root cannot be used as an anchor" \
        settled-by="a test that a doctrine-surface serving a frame is refused by dangling-relationship, plus praxis check clean at 48 of 48 with no surface naming the frame"
    claim "C4" text="every persona names the role it is, and a role names the phases it owns" \
        settled-by="praxis check over three role records and three persona surfaces, and a test that a role owning a phase no vocabulary declares is reported"

    depends-on "TS.260821.04 — the anchor vocabulary this corrects was flipped to refuse there"

    useful-alone "`the same engineer cannot self-approve` moves from agent-attested doctrine, the \
        bottom row of the enforcement table, to record-enforced, the top. It is the single rule this \
        plugin states most confidently and enforces least"

    attacks "S2"

    cut-from "ITER.260822.04, and the decision `what a persona serves` recorded there" \
        note="the decision is amended rather than replaced. It was correctly reasoned and wrongly \
            concluded, and an append that says so is worth more than a record that never shows the \
            wrong turn"

    principle-note "the personas resisted anchoring for the same reason the probes did in AM1: the \
        record had no word for what they are. AO1 called that a vocabulary gap rather than a \
        backlog. This is the third time, and the tell is the same both times — a surface that will \
        only anchor to the root is a surface the record cannot describe"

    proves "an attestation names an identity the engine can compare against who did the work"
    does-not-prove "that the reviewer reviewed. It refuses the one case that is definitionally not \
        a review, and a different mind rubber-stamping is still a rubber stamp"
}
